ective\n 13 | functions: the Frobenius norm, and the generalized Kullback-Leibler divergence.\n 14 | The latter is equivalent to Probabilistic Latent Semantic Indexing.\n 15 | \n 16 | The default parameters (n_samples / n_features / n_components) should make\n 17 | the example runnable in a couple of tens of seconds. You can try to\n 18 | increase the dimensions of the problem, but be aware that the time\n 19 | complexity is polynomial in NMF. In LDA, the time complexity is\n 20 | proportional to (n_samples * iterations).\n 21 | \n 22 | \"\"\"\n 23 | \n 24 | # Author: Olivier Grisel <olivier.grisel@ensta.org>\n 25 | #         Lars Buitinck\n 26 | #         Chyi-Kwei Yau <chyikwei.yau@gmail.com>\n 27 | # License: BSD 3 clause\n 28 | \n 29 | from time import time\n 30 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 31 | \n 32 | from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer, CountVectorizer\n 33 | from sklearn.decomposition import NMF, LatentDirichletAllocation\n 34 | from sklearn.datasets import fetch_20newsgroups\n 35 | \n 36 | n_samples = 2000\n 37 | n_features = 1000\n 38 | n_components = 10\n 39 | n_top_words = 20\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:_fit_coordinate_descent`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n417 | def _fit_coordinate_descent(\n418 |     X,\n419 |     W,\n420 |     H,\n421 |     tol=1e-4,\n422 |     max_iter=200,\n423 |     l1_reg_W=0,\n424 |     l1_reg_H=0,\n425 |     l2_reg_W=0,\n426 |     l2_reg_H=0,\n427 |     update_H=True,\n428 |     verbose=0,\n429 |     shuffle=False,\n430 |     random_state=None,\n431 | ):\n432 |     \"\"\"Compute Non-negative Matrix Factorization (NMF) with Coordinate Descent\n433 | \n434 |     The objective function is minimized with an alternating minimization of W\n435 |     and H. Each minimization is done with a cyclic (up to a permutation of the\n436 |     features) Coordinate Descent.\n437 | \n438 |     Parameters\n439 |     ----------\n440 |     X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n441 |         Constant matrix.\n442 | \n443 |     W : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n444 |         Initial guess for the solution.\n445 | \n446 |     H : array-like of shape (n_components, n_features)\n447 |         Initial guess for the solution.\n448 | \n449 |     tol : float, default=1e-4\n450 |         Tolerance of the stopping condition.\n451 | \n452 |     max_iter : int, default=200\n453 |         Maximum number of iterations before timing out.\n454 | \n455 |     l1_reg_W : float, default=0.\n456 |         L1 regularization parameter for W.\n457 | \n458 |     l1_reg_H : float, default=0.\n459 |         L1 regularization parameter for H.\n460 | \n461 |     l2_reg_W : float, default=0.\n462 |         L2 regularization parameter for W.\n463 | \n464 |     l2_reg_H : float, default=0.\n465 |         L2 regularization parameter for H.\n466 | \n467 |     update_H : bool, default=True\n468 |         Set to True, both W and H will be estimated from initial guesses.\n469 |         Set to False, only W will be estimated.\n470 | \n471 |     verbose : int, default=0\n472 |         The verbosity level.\n473 | \n474 |     shuffle : bool, default=False\n475 |         If true, randomize the order of coordinates in the CD solver.\n476 | \n477 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n478 |         Used to randomize the coordinates in the CD solver, when\n479 |         ``shuffle`` is set to ``True``. Pass an int for reproducible\n480 |         results across multiple function calls.\n481 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n482 | \n483 |     Returns\n484 |     -------\n485 |     W : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n486 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n487 | \n488 |     H : ndarray of shape (n_components, n_features)\n489 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n490 | \n491 |     n_iter : int\n492 |         The number of iterations done by the algorithm.\n493 | \n494 |     References\n495 |     ----------\n496 |     Cichocki, Andrzej, and Phan, Anh-Huy. \"Fast local algorithms for\n497 |     large scale nonnegative matrix and tensor factorizations.\"\n498 |     IEICE transactions on fundamentals of electronics, communications and\n499 |     computer sciences 92.3: 708-721, 2009.\n500 |     \"\"\"\n501 |     # so W and Ht are both in C order in memory\n502 |     Ht = check_array(H.T, order=\"C\")\n503 |     X = check_array(X, accept_sparse=\"csr\")\n504 | \n505 |     rng = check_random_state(random_state)\n506 | \n507 |     for n_iter in range(1, max_iter + 1):\n508 |         violation = 0.0\n509 | \n510 |         # Update W\n511 |         violation += _update_coordinate_descent(\n512 |             X, W, Ht, l1_reg_W, l2_reg_W, shuffle, rng\n513 |         )\n514 |         # Update H\n515 |         if update_H:\n516 |             violation += _update_coordinate_descent(\n517 |                 X.T, Ht, W, l1_reg_H, l2_reg_H, shuffle, rng\n518 |             )\n519 | \n520 |         if n_iter == 1:\n521 |             violation_init = violation\n522 | \n523 |         if violation_init == 0:\n524 |             break\n525 | \n526 |         if verbose:\n527 |             print(\"violation:\", violation / violation_init)\n528 | \n529 |         if violation / violation_init <= tol:\n530 |             if verbose:\n531 |                 print(\"Converged at iteration\", n_iter + 1)\n532 |             break\n533 | \n534 |     return W, Ht.T, n_iter\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/utils/estimator_checks.py:check_fit_non_negative`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n3517 | def check_fit_non_negative(name, estimator_orig):\n3518 |     # Check that proper warning is raised for non-negative X\n3519 |     # when tag requires_positive_X is present\n3520 |     X = np.array([[-1.0, 1], [-1.0, 1]])\n3521 |     y = np.array([1, 2])\n3522 |     estimator = clone(estimator_orig)\n3523 |     wi