         data through their quartiles.\n1225 |         The box extends from the Q1 to Q3 quartile values of the data,\n1226 |         with a line at the median (Q2). The whiskers extend from the edges\n1227 |         of box to show the range of the data. The position of the whiskers\n1228 |         is set by default to 1.5*IQR (IQR = Q3 - Q1) from the edges of the\n1229 |         box. Outlier points are those past the end of the whiskers.\n1230 | \n1231 |         For further details see Wikipedia's\n1232 |         entry for `boxplot <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Box_plot>`__.\n1233 | \n1234 |         A consideration when using this chart is that the box and the whiskers\n1235 |         can overlap, which is very common when plotting small sets of data.\n1236 | \n1237 |         Parameters\n1238 |         ----------\n1239 |         by : str or sequence\n1240 |             Column in the DataFrame to group by.\n1241 | \n1242 |             .. versionchanged:: 1.4.0\n1243 | \n1244 |                Previously, `by` is silently ignore and makes no groupings\n1245 | \n1246 |         **kwargs\n1247 |             Additional keywords are documented in\n1248 |             :meth:`DataFrame.plot`.\n1249 | \n1250 |         Returns\n1251 |         -------\n1252 |         :class:`matplotlib.axes.Axes` or numpy.ndarray of them\n1253 | \n1254 |         See Also\n1255 |         --------\n1256 |         DataFrame.boxplot: Another method to draw a box plot.\n1257 |         Series.plot.box: Draw a box plot from a Series object.\n1258 |         matplotlib.pyplot.boxplot: Draw a box plot in matplotlib.\n1259 | \n1260 |         Examples\n1261 |         --------\n1262 |         Draw a box plot from a DataFrame with four columns of randomly\n1263 |         generated data.\n1264 | \n1265 |         .. plot::\n1266 |             :context: close-figs\n1267 | \n1268 |             >>> data = np.random.randn(25, 4)\n1269 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame(data, columns=list('ABCD'))\n1270 |             >>> ax = df.plot.box()\n1271 | \n1272 |         You can also generate groupings if you specify the `by` parameter (which\n1273 |         can take a column name, or a list or tuple of column names):\n1274 | \n1275 |         .. versionchanged:: 1.4.0\n1276 | \n1277 |         .. plot::\n1278 |             :context: close-figs\n1279 | \n1280 |             >>> age_list = [8, 10, 12, 14, 72, 74, 76, 78, 20, 25, 30, 35, 60, 85]\n1281 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\"gender\": list(\"MMMMMMMMFFFFFF\"), \"age\": age_list})\n1282 |             >>> ax = df.plot.box(column=\"age\", by=\"gender\", figsize=(10, 8))\n1283 |         \"\"\"\n1284 |         return self(kind=\"box\", by=by, **kwargs)\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_core.py:PlotAccessor.area`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1458 | def area(self, x=None, y=None, **kwargs):\n1459 |         \"\"\"\n1460 |         Draw a stacked area plot.\n1461 | \n1462 |         An area plot displays quantitative data visually.\n1463 |         This function wraps the matplotlib area function.\n1464 | \n1465 |         Parameters\n1466 |         ----------\n1467 |         x : label or position, optional\n1468 |             Coordinates for the X axis. By default uses the index.\n1469 |         y : label or position, optional\n1470 |             Column to plot. By default uses all columns.\n1471 |         stacked : bool, default True\n1472 |             Area plots are stacked by default. Set to False to create a\n1473 |             unstacked plot.\n1474 |         **kwargs\n1475 |             Additional keyword arguments are documented in\n1476 |             :meth:`DataFrame.plot`.\n1477 | \n1478 |         Returns\n1479 |         -------\n1480 |         matplotlib.axes.Axes or numpy.ndarray\n1481 |             Area plot, or array of area plots if subplots is True.\n1482 | \n1483 |         See Also\n1484 |         --------\n1485 |         DataFrame.plot : Make plots of DataFrame using matplotlib / pylab.\n1486 | \n1487 |         Examples\n1488 |         --------\n1489 |         Draw an area plot based on basic business metrics:\n1490 | \n1491 |         .. plot::\n1492 |             :context: close-figs\n1493 | \n1494 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\n1495 |             ...     'sales': [3, 2, 3, 9, 10, 6],\n1496 |             ...     'signups': [5, 5, 6, 12, 14, 13],\n1497 |             ...     'visits': [20, 42, 28, 62, 81, 50],\n1498 |             ... }, index=pd.date_range(start='2018/01/01', end='2018/07/01',\n1499 |             ...                        freq='M'))\n1500 |             >>> ax = df.plot.area()\n1501 | \n1502 |         Area plots are stacked by default. To produce an unstacked plot,\n1503 |         pass ``stacked=False``:\n1504 | \n1505 |         .. plot::\n1506 |             :context: close-figs\n1507 | \n1508 |             >>> ax = df.plot.area(stacked=False)\n1509 | \n1510 |         Draw an area plot for a single column:\n1511 | \n1512 |         .. plot::\n1513 |             :context: close-figs\n1514 | \n1515 |             >>> ax = df.plot.area(y='sales')\n1516 | \n1517 |         Draw with a different `x`:\n1518 | \n1519 |         .. plot::\n1520 |             :context: close-figs\n1521 | \n1522 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\n1523 |             ...     'sales': [3, 2, 3],\n1524 |             ...     'visits': [20, 42, 28],\n1525 |             ...     'day': [1, 2, 3],\n1526 |             ... })\n1527 |             >>> ax = df.plot.area(x='day')\n1528 |         \"\"\"\n1529 |         return self(kind=\"area\", x=x, y=y, **kwargs)\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_core.py:PlotAccessor.pie`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1531 | def pie(self, **kwargs):\n1532 |         \"\"\"\n1533 |         Generate a pie plot.\n1534 | \n1535 |         A pie plot is a proportional representation of the numerical data in a\n1536 |         column. This function wraps :meth:`matplotlib.pyplot.pie` for the\n1537 |         specified column. If no column reference is passed and\n1538 |         ``subplots=True`` a pie plot is drawn for each numerical column\n1539 |         independently.\n1540 | \n1541 |         Parameters\n1542 |         ----------\n1543 |         y : int or label, optional\n1544 |             Label or position of the column to plot.\n1545 |             If not provided, ``subplots=True`` argument must be passed.\n1546 |         **kwargs\n1547 |             Keyword arguments to pass on to :meth:`DataFrame.plot`.\n1548 | \n1549 |         Returns\n1550 |         -------\n1551 |         matplotlib.axes.Axes or np.ndarray of them\n1552 |             A NumPy array is returned when `subplots` is True.\n1553 | \n1554 |         See Also\n1555 |         --------\n1556 |         Series.plot.pie : Generate a pie plot for a Series.\n1557 |         DataFrame.plot : Make plots of a DataFrame.\n1558 | \n1559 |         Examples\n1560 |         --------\n1561 |         In the example below we have a DataFrame with the information about\n1562 |         planet's mass and radius. We pass the 'mass' column to the\n1563 |         pie function to get a pie plot.\n1564 | \n1565 |         .. plot::\n1566 |             :context: close-figs\n1567 | \n1568 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({'mass': [0.330, 4.87 , 5.97],\n1569 |             ...                    'radius': [2439.7, 6051.8, 6378.1]},\n1570 |             ...                   index=['Mercury', 'Venus', 'Earth'])\n1571 |             >>> plot = df.plot.pie(y='mass', figsize=(5, 5))\n1572 | \n1573 |         .. plot::\n1574 |             :context: close-figs\n1575 | \n1576 |             >>> plot = df.plot.pie(subplots=True, figsize=(11, 6))\n1577 |         \"\"\"\n1578 |         if (\n1579 |             isinstance(self._parent, ABCDataFrame)\n1580 |             and kwargs.get(\"y\", None) is None\n1581 |             and not kwargs.get(\"subplots\", False)\n1582 |         ):\n1583 |             raise ValueError(\"pie requires either y column or 'subplots=True'\")\n1584 |         return self(kind=\"pie\", **kwargs)\n```\n\nFound file `pandas/core/exchange/dataframe.py`.\nFound file `pandas/plotting/__init__.py`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nHint: Use more detailed query to get the full content of some if needed.\n"}, {"content": "Based on the search results, the primary module involved in the plotting functionality is `pandas/plotting/_core.py`, specifically the `PlotAccessor` class and its `plot` method. The `DataFrame` class from `pandas/core/frame.py` is also relevant because it includes the `plot` accessor.\n\nNext, I'll search for the specific `plot` method in `pandas/plotting/_core.py` to understand its implementation and where modifications might be needed.\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>pandas/plotting/_core.py</parameter>\n<parameter=search_terms>\n[\"plot\", \"subplots\"]\n</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\npandas/plotting/_core.py:boxplot_frame_groupby\npandas/plotting/_core.py:PlotAccessor.pie\npandas/plotting/_core.py:PlotAccessor:13\npandas/plotting/_core.py:PlotAccessor.line\n##Searching for term \"plot\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/util/_doctools.py:TablePlotter.plot`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `plot`.\n```\n 44 | def plot(self, left, right, labels=None, vertical: bool = True):\n 45 |         \"\"\"\n 46 |         Plot left / right DataFrames in specified layout.\n 47 | \n 48 |         Parameters\n 49 |         ----------\n 50 |         left : list of DataFrames before operation is applied\n 51 |         right : DataFrame of operation result\n 52 |         labels : list of str to be drawn as titles of left DataFrames\n 53 |         vertical : bool, default True\n 54 |             If True, use vertical layout. If False, use horizontal layout.\n 55 |         \"\"\"\n 56 |         import matplotlib.gridspec as gridspec\n 57 |         import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 58 | \n 59 |         if not isinstance(left, list):\n 60 |             left = [left]\n 61 |         left = [self._conv(df) for df in left]\n 62 |         right = self._conv(right)\n 63 | \n 64 |         hcells, vcells = self._get_cells(left, right, vertical)\n 65 | \n 66 |         if vertical:\n 67 |             figsize = self.cell_width * hcells, self.cell_height * vcells\n 68 |         else:\n 69 |             # include margin for titles\n 70 |             figsize = self.cell_width * hcells, self.cell_height * vcells\n 71 |         fig = plt.figure(figsize=figsize)\n 72 | \n 73 |         if vertical:\n 74 |             gs = gridspec.GridSpec(len(left), hcells)\n 75 |             # left\n 76 |             max_left_cols = max(self._shape(df)[1] for df in left)\n 77 |             max_left_rows = max(self._shape(df)[0] for df in left)\n 78 |             for i, (l, label) in enumerate(zip(left, labels)):\n 79 |                 ax = fig.add_subplot(gs[i, 0:max_left_cols])\n 80 |                 self._make_table(ax, l, title=label, height=1.0 / max_left_rows)\n 81 |             # right\n 82 |             ax = plt.subplot(gs[:, max_left_cols:])\n 83 |             self._make_table(ax, right, title=\"Result\", height=1.05 / vcells)\n 84 |             fig.subplots_adjust(top=0.9, bottom=0.05, left=0.05, right=0.95)\n 85 |         else:\n 86 |             max_rows = max(self._shape(df)[0] for df in left + [right])\n 87 |             height = 1.0 / np.max(max_rows)\n 88 |             gs = gridspec.GridSpec(1, hcells)\n 89 |             # left\n 90 |             i = 0\n 91 |             for df, label in zip(left, labels):\n 92 |                 sp = self._shape(df)\n 93 |                 ax = fig.add_subplot(gs[0, i : i + sp[1]])\n 94 |                 self._make_table(ax, df, title=label, height=height)\n 95 |                 i += sp[1]\n 96 |             # right\n 97 |             ax = plt.subplot(gs[0, i:])\n 98 |             self._make_table(ax, right, title=\"Result\", height=height)\n 99 |             fig.subplots_adjust(top=0.85, bottom=0.05, left=0.05, right=0.95)\n100 | \n101 |         return fig\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/__init__.py:plot`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `plot`.\n```\n58 | def plot(data, kind, **kwargs):\n59 |     # Importing pyplot at the top of the file (before the converters are\n60 |     # registered) causes problems in matplotlib 2 (converters seem to not\n61 |     # work)\n62 |     import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n63 | \n64 |     if kwargs.pop(\"reuse_plot\", False):\n65 |         ax = kwargs.get(\"ax\")\n66 |         if ax is None and len(plt.get_fignums()) > 0:\n67 |             with plt.rc_context():\n68 |                 ax = plt.gca()\n69 |             kwargs[\"ax\"] = getattr(ax, \"left_ax\", ax)\n70 |     plot_obj = PLOT_CLASSES[kind](data, **kwargs)\n71 |     plot_obj.generate()\n72 |     plot_obj.draw()\n73 |     return plot_obj.result\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"subplots\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/tools.py:create_subplots`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n132 | def create_subplots(\n133 |     naxes: int,\n134 |     sharex: bool = False,\n135 |     sharey: bool = False,\n136 |     squeeze: bool = True,\n137 |     subplot_kw=None,\n138 |     ax=None,\n139 |     layout=None,\n140 |     layout_type: str = \"box\",\n141 |     **fig_kw,\n142 | ):\n143 |     \"\"\"\n144 |     Create a figure with a set of subplots already made.\n145 | \n146 |     This utility wrapper makes it convenient to create common layouts of\n147 |     subplots, including the enclosing figure object, in a single call.\n148 | \n149 |     Parameters\n150 |     ----------\n151 |     naxes : int\n152 |       Number of required axes. Exceeded axes are set invisible. Default is\n153 |       nrows * ncols.\n154 | \n155 |     sharex : bool\n156 |       If True, the X axis will be shared amongst all subplots.\n157 | \n158 |     sharey : bool\n159 |       If True, the Y axis will be shared amongst all subplots.\n160 | \n161 |     squeeze : bool\n162 | \n163 |       If True, extra dimensions are squeezed out from the returned axis object:\n164 |         - if only one subplot is constructed (nrows=ncols=1), the resulting\n165 |         single Axis object is returned as a scalar.\n166 |         - for Nx1 or 1xN subplots, the returned object is a 1-d numpy object\n167 |         array of Axis objects are returned as numpy 1-d arrays.\n168 |         - for NxM subplots with N>1 and M>1 are returned as a 2d array.\n169 | \n170 |       If False, no squeezing is done: the returned axis object is always\n171 |       a 2-d array containing Axis instances, even if it ends up being 1x1.\n172 | \n173 |     subplot_kw : dict\n174 |       Dict with keywords passed to the add_subplot() call used to create each\n175 |       subplots.\n176 | \n177 |     ax : Matplotlib axis object, optional\n178 | \n179 |     layout : tuple\n180 |       Number of rows and columns of the subplot grid.\n181 |       If not specified, calculated from naxes and layout_type\n182 | \n183 |     layout_type : {'box', 'horizontal', 'vertical'}, default 'box'\n184 |       Specify how to layout the subplot grid.\n185 | \n186 |     fig_kw : Other keyword arguments to be passed to the figure() call.\n187 |         Note that all keywords not recognized above will be\n188 |         automatically included here.\n189 | \n190 |     Returns\n191 |     -------\n192 |     fig, ax : tuple\n193 |       - fig is the Matplotlib Figure object\n194 |       - ax can be either a single axis object or an array of axis objects if\n195 |       more than one subplot was created.  The dimensions of the resulting array\n196 |       can be controlled with the squeeze keyword, see above.\n197 | \n198 |     Examples\n199 |     --------\n200 |     x = np.linspace(0, 2*np.pi, 400)\n201 |     y = np.sin(x**2)\n202 | \n203 |     # Just a figure and one subplot\n204 |     f, ax = plt.subplots()\n205 |     ax.plot(x, y)\n206 |     ax.set_title('Simple plot')\n207 | \n208 |     # Two subplots, unpack the output array immediately\n209 |     f, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(1, 2, sharey=True)\n210 |     ax1.plot(x, y)\n211 |     ax1.set_title('Sharing Y axis')\n212 |     ax2.scatter(x, y)\n213 | \n214 |     # Four polar axes\n215 |     plt.subplots(2, 2, subplot_kw=dict(polar=True))\n216 |     \"\"\"\n217 |     import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n218 | \n219 |     if subplot_kw is None:\n220 |         subplot_kw = {}\n221 | \n222 |     if ax is None:\n223 |         fig = plt.figure(**fig_kw)\n224 |     else:\n225 |         if is_list_like(ax):\n226 |             if squeeze:\n227 |                 ax = flatten_axes(ax)\n228 |             if layout is not None:\n229 |                 warnings.warn(\n230 |                     \"When passing multiple axes, layout keyword is ignored.\",\n231 |                     UserWarning,\n232 |                 )\n233 |             if sharex or sharey:\n234 |                 warnings.warn(\n235 |                     \"When passing multiple axes, sharex and sharey \"\n236 |                     \"are ignored. These settings must be specified when creating axes.\",\n237 |                     UserWarning,\n238 |                     stacklevel=find_stack_level(),\n239 |                 )\n240 |             if ax.size == naxes:\n241 |                 fig = ax.flat[0].get_figure()\n242 |                 return fig, ax\n243 |             else:\n244 |                 raise ValueError(\n245 |                     f\"The number of passed axes must be {naxes}, the \"\n246 |                     \"same as the output plot\"\n247 |                 )\n248 | \n249 |         fig = ax.get_figure()\n250 |         # if ax is passed and a number of subplots is 1, return ax as it is\n251 |         if naxes == 1:\n252 |             if squeeze:\n253 |                 return fig, ax\n254 |             else:\n255 |                 return fig, flatten_axes(ax)\n256 |         else:\n257 |             warnings.warn(\n258 |                 \"To output multiple subplots, the figure containing \"\n259 |                 \"the passed axes is being cleared.\",\n260 |                 UserWarning,\n261 |                 stacklevel=find_stack_level(),\n262 |             )\n263 |             fig.clear()\n264 | \n265 |     nrows, ncols = _get_layout(naxes, layout=layout, layout_type=layout_type)\n266 |     nplots = nrows * ncols\n267 | \n268 |     # Create empty object array to hold all axes.  It's easiest to make it 1-d\n269 |     # so we can just append subplots upon creation, and then\n270 |     axarr = np.empty(nplots, dtype=object)\n271 | \n272 |     # Create first subplot separately, so we can share it if requested\n273 |     ax0 = fig.add_subplot(nrows, ncols, 1, **subplot_kw)\n274 | \n275 |     if sharex:\n276 |         subplot_kw[\"sharex\"] = ax0\n277 |     if sharey:\n278 |         subplot_kw[\"sharey\"] = ax0\n279 |     axarr[0] = ax0\n280 | \n281 |     # Note off-by-one counting because add_subplot uses the MATLAB 1-based\n282 |     # convention.\n283 |     for i in range(1, nplots):\n284 |         kwds = subplot_kw.copy()\n285 |         # Set sharex and sharey to None for blank/dummy axes, these can\n286 |         # interfere with proper axis limits on the visible axes if\n287 |         # they share axes e.g. issue #7528\n288 |         if i >= naxes:\n289 |             kwds[\"sharex\"] = None\n290 |             kwds[\"sharey\"] = None\n291 |         ax = fig.add_subplot(nrows, ncols, i + 1, **kwds)\n292 |         axarr[i] = ax\n293 | \n294 |     if naxes != nplots:\n295 |         for ax in axarr[naxes:]:\n296 |             ax.set_visible(False)\n297 | \n298 |     handle_shared_axes(axarr, nplots, naxes, nrows, ncols, sharex, sharey)\n299 | \n300 |     if squeeze:\n301 |         # Reshape the array to have the final desired dimension (nrow,ncol),\n302 |         # though discarding unneeded dimensions that equal 1.  If we only have\n303 |         # one subplot, just return it instead of a 1-element array.\n304 |         if nplots == 1:\n305 |             axes = axarr[0]\n306 |         else:\n307 |             axes = axarr.reshape(nrows, ncols).squeeze()\n308 |     else:\n309 |         # returned axis array will be always 2-d, even if nrows=ncols=1\n310 |         axes = axarr.reshape(nrows, ncols)\n311 | \n312 |     return fig, axes\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:MPLPlot._setup_subplots`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n372 | def _setup_subplots(self):\n373 |         if self.subplots:\n374 |             fig, axes = create_subplots(\n375 |                 naxes=self.nseries,\n376 |                 sharex=self.sharex,\n377 |                 sharey=self.sharey,\n378 |                 figsize=self.figsize,\n379 |                 ax=self.ax,\n380 |                 layout=self.layout,\n381 |                 layout_type=self._layout_type,\n382 |             )\n383 |         else:\n384 |             if self.ax is None:\n385 |                 fig = self.plt.figure(figsize=self.figsize)\n386 |                 axes = fig.add_subplot(111)\n387 |             else:\n388 |                 fig = self.ax.get_figure()\n389 |                 if self.figsize is not None:\n390 |                     fig.set_size_inches(self.figsize)\n391 |                 axes = self.ax\n392 | \n393 |         axes = flatten_axes(axes)\n394 | \n395 |         valid_log = {False, True, \"sym\", None}\n396 |         input_log = {self.logx, self.logy, self.loglog}\n397 |         if input_log - valid_log:\n398 |             invalid_log = next(iter(input_log - valid_log))\n399 |             raise ValueError(\n400 |                 f\"Boolean, None and 'sym' are valid options, '{invalid_log}' is given.\"\n401 |             )\n402 | \n403 |         if self.logx is True or self.loglog is True:\n404 |             [a.set_xscale(\"log\") for a in axes]\n405 |         elif self.logx == \"sym\" or self.loglog == \"sym\":\n406 |             [a.set_xscale(\"symlog\") for a in axes]\n407 | \n408 |         if self.logy is True or self.loglog is True:\n409 |             [a.set_yscale(\"log\") for a in axes]\n410 |         elif self.logy == \"sym\" or self.loglog == \"sym\":\n411 |             [a.set_yscale(\"symlog\") for a in axes]\n412 | \n413 |         self.fig = fig\n414 |         self.axes = axes\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:MPLPlot._adorn_subplots`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n544 | def _adorn_subplots(self):\n545 |         \"\"\"Common post process unrelated to data\"\"\"\n546 |         if len(self.axes) > 0:\n547 |             all_axes = self._get_subplots()\n548 |             nrows, ncols = self._get_axes_layout()\n549 |             handle_shared_axes(\n550 |                 axarr=all_axes,\n551 |                 nplots=len(all_axes),\n552 |                 naxes=nrows * ncols,\n553 |                 nrows=nrows,\n554 |                 ncols=ncols,\n555 |                 sharex=self.sharex,\n556 |                 sharey=self.sharey,\n557 |             )\n558 | \n559 |         for ax in self.axes:\n560 |             if self.yticks is not None:\n561 |                 ax.set_yticks(self.yticks)\n562 | \n563 |             if self.xticks is not None:\n564 |                 ax.set_xticks(self.xticks)\n565 | \n566 |             if self.ylim is not None:\n567 |                 ax.set_ylim(self.ylim)\n568 | \n569 |             if self.xlim is not None:\n570 |                 ax.set_xlim(self.xlim)\n571 | \n572 |             # GH9093, currently Pandas does not show ylabel, so if users provide\n573 |             # ylabel will set it as ylabel in the plot.\n574 |             if self.ylabel is not None:\n575 |                 ax.set_ylabel(pprint_thing(self.ylabel))\n576 | \n577 |             ax.grid(self.grid)\n578 | \n579 |         if self.title:\n580 |             if self.subplots:\n581 |                 if is_list_like(self.title):\n582 |                     if len(self.title) != self.nseries:\n583 |                         raise ValueError(\n584 |                             \"The length of `title` must equal the number \"\n585 |                             \"of columns if using `title` of type `list` \"\n586 |                             \"and `subplots=True`.\\n\"\n587 |                             f\"length of title = {len(self.title)}\\n\"\n588 |                             f\"number of columns = {self.nseries}\"\n589 |                         )\n590 | \n591 |                     for (ax, title) in zip(self.axes, self.title):\n592 |                         ax.set_title(title)\n593 |                 else:\n594 |                     self.fig.suptitle(self.title)\n595 |             else:\n596 |                 if is_list_like(self.title):\n597 |                     msg = (\n598 |                         \"Using `title` of type `list` is not supported \"\n599 |                         \"unless `subplots=True` is passed\"\n600 |                     )\n601 |                     raise ValueError(msg)\n602 |                 self.axes[0].set_title(self.title)\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_core.py:boxplot_frame_groupby`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n526 | def boxplot_frame_groupby(\n527 |     grouped,\n528 |     subplots=True,\n529 |     column=None,\n530 |     fontsize=None,\n531 |     rot=0,\n532 |     grid=True,\n533 |     ax=None,\n534 |     figsize=None,\n535 |     layout=None,\n536 |     sharex=False,\n537 |     sharey=True,\n538 |     backend=None,\n539 |     **kwargs,\n540 | ):\n541 |     \"\"\"\n542 |     Make box plots from DataFrameGroupBy data.\n543 | \n544 |     Parameters\n545 |     ----------\n546 |     grouped : Grouped DataFrame\n547 |     subplots : bool\n548 |         * ``False`` - no subplots will be used\n549 |         * ``True`` - create a subplot for each group.\n550 | \n551 |     column : column name or list of names, or vector\n552 |         Can be any valid input to groupby.\n553 |     fontsize : int or str\n554 |     rot : label rotation angle\n555 |     grid : Setting this to True will show the grid\n556 |     ax : Matplotlib axis object, default None\n557 |     figsize : A tuple (width, height) in inches\n558 |     layout : tuple (optional)\n559 |         The layout of the plot: (rows, columns).\n560 |     sharex : bool, default False\n561 |         Whether x-axes will be shared among subplots.\n562 |     sharey : bool, default True\n563 |         Whether y-axes will be shared among subplots.\n564 |     backend : str, default None\n565 |         Backend to use instead of the backend specified in the option\n566 |         ``plotting.backend``. For instance, 'matplotlib'. Alternatively, to\n567 |         specify the ``plotting.backend`` for the whole session, set\n568 |         ``pd.options.plotting.backend``.\n569 | \n570 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0.0\n571 | \n572 |     **kwargs\n573 |         All other plotting keyword arguments to be passed to\n574 |         matplotlib's boxplot function.\n575 | \n576 |     Returns\n577 |     -------\n578 |     dict of key/value = group key/DataFrame.boxplot return value\n579 |     or DataFrame.boxplot return value in case subplots=figures=False\n580 | \n581 |     Examples\n582 |     --------\n583 |     You can create boxplots for grouped data and show them as separate subplots:\n584 | \n585 |     .. plot::\n586 |         :context: close-figs\n587 | \n588 |         >>> import itertools\n589 |         >>> tuples = [t for t in itertools.product(range(1000), range(4))]\n590 |         >>> index = pd.MultiIndex.from_tuples(tuples, names=['lvl0', 'lvl1'])\n591 |         >>> data = np.random.randn(len(index),4)\n592 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame(data, columns=list('ABCD'), index=index)\n593 |         >>> grouped = df.groupby(level='lvl1')\n594 |         >>> grouped.boxplot(rot=45, fontsize=12, figsize=(8,10))  # doctest: +SKIP\n595 | \n596 |     The ``subplots=False`` option shows the boxplots in a single figure.\n597 | \n598 |     .. plot::\n599 |         :context: close-figs\n600 | \n601 |         >>> grouped.boxplot(subplots=False, rot=45, fontsize=12)  # doctest: +SKIP\n602 |     \"\"\"\n603 |     plot_backend = _get_plot_backend(backend)\n604 |     return plot_backend.boxplot_frame_groupby(\n605 |         grouped,\n606 |         subplots=subplots,\n607 |         column=column,\n608 |         fontsize=fontsize,\n609 |         rot=rot,\n610 |         grid=grid,\n611 |         ax=ax,\n612 |         figsize=figsize,\n613 |         layout=layout,\n614 |         sharex=sharex,\n615 |         sharey=sharey,\n616 |         **kwargs,\n617 |     )\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_core.py:PlotAccessor.line`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1034 | def line(self, x=None, y=None, **kwargs):\n1035 |         \"\"\"\n1036 |         Plot Series or DataFrame as lines.\n1037 | \n1038 |         This function is useful to plot lines using DataFrame's values\n1039 |         as coordinates.\n1040 |         \"\"\"\n1041 |         return self(kind=\"line\", x=x, y=y, **kwargs)\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_core.py:PlotAccessor.pie`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1531 | def pie(self, **kwargs):\n1532 |         \"\"\"\n1533 |         Generate a pie plot.\n1534 | \n1535 |         A pie plot is a proportional representation of the numerical data in a\n1536 |         column. This function wraps :meth:`matplotlib.pyplot.pie` for the\n1537 |         specified column. If no column reference is passed and\n1538 |         ``subplots=True`` a pie plot is drawn for each numerical column\n1539 |         independently.\n1540 | \n1541 |         Parameters\n1542 |         ----------\n1543 |         y : int or label, optional\n1544 |             Label or position of the column to plot.\n1545 |             If not provided, ``subplots=True`` argument must be passed.\n1546 |         **kwargs\n1547 |             Keyword arguments to pass on to :meth:`DataFrame.plot`.\n1548 | \n1549 |         Returns\n1550 |         -------\n1551 |         matplotlib.axes.Axes or np.ndarray of them\n1552 |             A NumPy array is returned when `subplots` is True.\n1553 | \n1554 |         See Also\n1555 |         --------\n1556 |         Series.plot.pie : Generate a pie plot for a Series.\n1557 |         DataFrame.plot : Make plots of a DataFrame.\n1558 | \n1559 |         Examples\n1560 |         --------\n1561 |         In the example below we have a DataFrame with the information about\n1562 |         planet's mass and radius. We pass the 'mass' column to the\n1563 |         pie function to get a pie plot.\n1564 | \n1565 |         .. plot::\n1566 |             :context: close-figs\n1567 | \n1568 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({'mass': [0.330, 4.87 , 5.97],\n1569 |             ...                    'radius': [2439.7, 6051.8, 6378.1]},\n1570 |             ...                   index=['Mercury', 'Venus', 'Earth'])\n1571 |             >>> plot = df.plot.pie(y='mass', figsize=(5, 5))\n1572 | \n1573 |         .. plot::\n1574 |             :context: close-figs\n1575 | \n1576 |             >>> plot = df.plot.pie(subplots=True, figsize=(11, 6))\n1577 |         \"\"\"\n1578 |         if (\n1579 |             isinstance(self._parent, ABCDataFrame)\n1580 |             and kwargs.get(\"y\", None) is None\n1581 |             and not kwargs.get(\"subplots\", False)\n1582 |         ):\n1583 |             raise ValueError(\"pie requires either y column or 'subplots=True'\")\n1584 |         return self(kind=\"pie\", **kwargs)\n```\n"}, {"content": "Based on the provided search results, the key areas for implementing the requested feature are located in several files within the `pandas/plotting` directory. Specifically, the `_core.py` and `_matplotlib/core.py` files contain the primary logic for handling the `plot` method and subplots.\n\nHere are the relevant locations:\n\n```\npandas/core/frame.py\nclass: DataFrame\nfunction: plot\n\npandas/plotting/_core.py\nclass: PlotAccessor\nfunction: plot\n\npandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py\nclass: MPLPlot\nfunction: _setup_subplots\n\npandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py\nclass: MPLPlot\nfunction: _adorn_subplots\n\npandas/plotting/_matplotlib/__init__.py\nfunction: plot\n```\n\nThese locations are crucial for modifying the behavior of the `plot` method to accommodate grouping columns on the same subplot.\n\n<finish></finish>", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 68673, "completion_tokens": 561}, "time": 73.6795060634613}]}}
{"instance_id": "scikit-learn__scikit-learn-14800", "found_files": [["sklearn/datasets/_openml.py"]], "found_modules": [["sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_download_data_to_bunch", "sklearn/externals/_arff.py:ArffDecoder._decode", "sklearn/naive_bayes.py:ComplementNB"]], "found_entities": [["sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_download_data_to_bunch", "sklearn/externals/_arff.py:ArffDecoder._decode.stream", "sklearn/naive_bayes.py:ComplementNB._joint_log_likelihood"]], "raw_output_loc": ["Based on the search results, the key parts of the feature implementation or modification should focus on the `_get_data_description_by_id` function and the `fetch_openml` function where the data is downloaded and potentially verified. Additionally, we need to look into the `_download_data_to_bunch` function since it handles the downloading of the ARFF data.\n\nHere are the locations for potential modifications:\n\n```\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 430\nclass: _get_data_description_by_id\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 671\nclass: fetch_openml\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 819\nfunction: _download_data_to_bunch\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 881\nfunction: _download_data_to_bunch\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 891\nfunction: _download_data_to_bunch\n```"], "meta_data": {"repo": "scikit-learn/scikit-learn", "base_commit": "acb8ac5145cfd88fdbd2d381b34883b2c212c8c5", "problem_statement": "Validate MD5 checksum of downloaded ARFF in fetch_openml\n`fetch_openml` downloads the `data_set_description` from openml.org which includes an `md5_checksum` field. This should correspond to the MD5 checksum of the downloaded ARFF data. We should (optionally?) use `hashlib` to verify that the MD5 matches.\n", "patch": "diff --git a/doc/whats_new/v0.24.rst b/doc/whats_new/v0.24.rst\nindex a647de6dac628..ba137fb76698f 100644\n--- a/doc/whats_new/v0.24.rst\n+++ b/doc/whats_new/v0.24.rst\n@@ -224,6 +224,11 @@ Changelog\n   :meth:`tree.DecisionTreeRegressor.fit`, and has not effect.\n   :pr:`17614` by :user:`Juan Carlos Alfaro Jim\u00e9nez <alfaro96>`.\n \n+:mod:`sklearn.datasets`\n+.......................\n+- |Feature| :func:`datasets.fetch_openml` now validates md5checksum of arff\n+  files downloaded or cached to ensure data integrity.\n+  :pr:`14800` by :user:`Shashank Singh <shashanksingh28>` and `Joel Nothman`_.\n \n Code and Documentation Contributors\n -----------------------------------\ndiff --git a/sklearn/datasets/_openml.py b/sklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nindex 6b537c09c59ad..d2ce5d471f8f7 100644\n--- a/sklearn/datasets/_openml.py\n+++ b/sklearn/datasets/_openml.py\n@@ -2,6 +2,7 @@\n import json\n import os\n import shutil\n+import hashlib\n from os.path import join\n from warnings import warn\n from contextlib import closing\n@@ -492,7 +493,8 @@ def _load_arff_response(\n     url: str,\n     data_home: Optional[str],\n     return_type, encode_nominal: bool,\n-    parse_arff: Callable[[ArffContainerType], Tuple]\n+    parse_arff: Callable[[ArffContainerType], Tuple],\n+    md5_checksum: str\n ) -> Tuple:\n     \"\"\"Load arff data with url and parses arff response with parse_arff\"\"\"\n     response = _open_openml_url(url, data_home)\n@@ -500,10 +502,32 @@ def _load_arff_response(\n     with closing(response):\n         # Note that if the data is dense, no reading is done until the data\n         # generator is iterated.\n-        arff = _arff.load((line.decode('utf-8') for line in response),\n+        actual_md5_checksum = hashlib.md5()\n+\n+        def _stream_checksum_generator(response):\n+            for line in response:\n+                actual_md5_checksum.update(line)\n+                yield line.decode('utf-8')\n+\n+        stream = _stream_checksum_generator(response)\n+\n+        arff = _arff.load(stream,\n                           return_type=return_type,\n                           encode_nominal=encode_nominal)\n-        return parse_arff(arff)\n+\n+        parsed_arff = parse_arff(arff)\n+\n+        # consume remaining stream, if early exited\n+        for _ in stream:\n+            pass\n+\n+        if actual_md5_checksum.hexdigest() != md5_checksum:\n+            raise ValueError(\"md5 checksum of local file for \" + url +\n+                             \" does not match description. \"\n+                             \"Downloaded file could have been modified / \"\n+                             \"corrupted, clean cache and retry...\")\n+\n+        return parsed_arff\n \n \n def _download_data_to_bunch(\n@@ -515,7 +539,8 @@ def _download_data_to_bunch(\n     features_list: List,\n     data_columns: List[int],\n     target_columns: List,\n-    shape: Optional[Tuple[int, int]]\n+    shape: Optional[Tuple[int, int]],\n+    md5_checksum: str\n ):\n     \"\"\"Download OpenML ARFF and convert to Bunch of data\n     \"\"\"\n@@ -609,7 +634,8 @@ def postprocess(X, y, nominal_attributes):\n         _load_arff_response)(url, data_home,\n                              return_type=return_type,\n                              encode_nominal=not as_frame,\n-                             parse_arff=parse_arff)\n+                             parse_arff=parse_arff,\n+                             md5_checksum=md5_checksum)\n     X, y, frame, nominal_attributes = postprocess(*out)\n \n     return Bunch(data=X, target=y, frame=frame,\n@@ -883,7 +909,9 @@ def fetch_openml(\n                                     as_frame=as_frame,\n                                     features_list=features_list, shape=shape,\n                                     target_columns=target_columns,\n-                                    data_columns=data_columns)\n+                                    data_columns=data_columns,\n+                                    md5_checksum=data_description[\n+                                        \"md5_checksum\"])\n \n     if return_X_y:\n         return bunch.data, bunch.target\n"}, "usage": {"cost($)": "0", "prompt_tokens": 23966, "completion_tokens": 397}, "loc_trajs": {"trajs": [{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "A chat between a curious user and an artificial intelligence assistant. The assistant gives helpful, detailed answers to the user's questions.\nThe assistant can use a Python environment with <execute_ipython>, e.g.:\n<execute_ipython>\nprint(\"Hello World!\")\n</execute_ipython>\n\nApart from the standard Python library, the assistant can also use the following functions (already imported) in <execute_ipython> environment:\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms: Optional[List[str]] = None, line_nums: Optional[List] = None, file_path_or_pattern: Optional[str] = '**/*.py') -> str:\n    Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n    This function supports retrieving the complete content of a code entity,\n    searching for code entities such as classes or functions by keywords, or locating specific lines within a file.\n    It also supports filtering searches based on a file path or file pattern.\n    Note:\n    1. If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n    - If a term is formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') ,\n    or just 'file_path', the corresponding complete code is retrieved or file content is retrieved.\n    - If a term matches a file, class, or function name, matched entities are retrieved.\n    - If there is no match with any module name, it attempts to find code snippets that likely contain the term.\n    2. If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets at the specified lines within the file defined by\n    `file_path_or_pattern`.\n    Args:\n    search_terms (Optional[List[str]]): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase.\n    Terms can be formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' to search for a specific module or entity within a file\n    (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') or as 'file_path' to retrieve the complete content\n    of a file. This can also include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments.\n    line_nums (Optional[List[int]]): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file.\n    When provided, `file_path_or_pattern` must specify a valid file path.\n    file_path_or_pattern (Optional[str]): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results\n    to particular files or directories. Defaults to '**/*.py', meaning all Python files are searched by default.\n    If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n    Returns:\n    str: The search results, which may include code snippets, matching entities, or complete file content.\n    Example Usage:\n    # Search for the full content of a specific file\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py'])\n    # Search for a specific function\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n    # Search for specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\n    result = search_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n    # Combined search for a module name and within a specific file pattern\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"], file_path_or_pattern=\"src/**/*.py\")\n\n\nFunction Usage Guidelines:\n- Issue Analysis and Retrieval: Begin by analyzing the issue and extracting relevant information for retrieval. Use the functions `search_in_repo` to gather more insights about the issue.\n- Accessing Files and Code: Use `get_file_structures` or `search_class_structures` to gain an overview of the files or classes; Call `get_file_content` to access the content of specific file relevant to the issue, and employ `search_class` or `search_method` to retrieve specific modules when you know the specific names of these modules.\n\n- Repository Overview: Use `get_repo_structure` to gain an overview of the repository. This aids in independently searching for related files or analyzing results from previous steps. However, this tool is relatively expensive, use other tools to search files, classes or methods if you know their potential names.\n- File Limitations: You may search as many files as necessary, but make sure not include noise.\n- Exclusions: Do not include any existing test case files in your findings.\n- Evaluate and prioritize findings from different search methods, reranking the answer when you give final output.\nIMPORTANT:\n- Parameter Accuracy: Ensure that the parameters used for each tool call are accurate, avoiding assumptions.\n\nResponses should be concise.\nThe assistant should attempt fewer things at a time instead of putting too many commands OR too much code in one \"execute\" block.\nInclude ONLY ONE <execute_ipython> per response, unless the assistant is finished with the task or needs more input or action from the user in order to proceed.\nIf the assistant is finished with the task you MUST include <finish></finish> in your response.\nIMPORTANT: Execute code using <execute_ipython> whenever possible.\nThe assistant should utilize full file paths to prevent path-related errors.\nThe assistant must avoid apologies and thanks in its responses.\nYou have access to the following functions:\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #1: finish ----\nDescription: Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task.\nNo parameters are required for this function.\n---- END FUNCTION #1 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #2: search_code_snippets ----\nDescription: Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n\nParameters:\n  (1) search_terms (array, optional): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (2) line_nums (array, optional): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (3) file_path_or_pattern (string, optional): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n---- END FUNCTION #2 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #3: get_entity_contents ----\nDescription: \nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n\nParameters:\n  (1) entity_names (array, required): A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n---- END FUNCTION #3 ----\n\n\nIf you choose to call a function ONLY reply in the following format with NO suffix:\n\n<function=example_function_name>\n<parameter=example_parameter_1>value_1</parameter>\n<parameter=example_parameter_2>\nThis is the value for the second parameter\nthat can span\nmultiple lines\n</parameter>\n</function>\n\n<IMPORTANT>\nReminder:\n- Function calls MUST follow the specified format, start with <function= and end with </function>\n- Required parameters MUST be specified\n- Only call one function at a time\n- You may provide optional reasoning for your function call in natural language BEFORE the function call, but NOT after.\n- If there is no function call available, answer the question like normal with your current knowledge and do not tell the user about function calls\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "\nGiven the following GitHub issue description, your objective is to localize the specific files, classes or functions, and lines of code that are relevant to the solution for the issue. More specifically, these issues are all \"feature request\" issues, which means that you need to find the locations the feature described in the issue.\n\nFollow these steps to localize the issue:\n## Step 1: Categorize and Extract Key Problem Information\n - Classify the issue description into the following categories:\n    Requested feature description, behavior with out this feature, expected behavior with the feature, and additional context.\n - Identify modules in the 'scikit-learn' package mentioned in each category.\n - Use extracted keywords and line numbers to search for relevant code references for additional context.\n\n## Step 2: Locate Referenced Modules\n- Accurately determine specific modules\n    - Explore the repo to familiarize yourself with its structure.\n    - Analyze the described workflow to identify specific modules or components being referenced.\n- Pay special attention to distinguishing between modules with similar names using context and described workflow.\n- Output Format for collected relevant modules:\n    - Use the format: 'file_path:QualifiedName'\n    - E.g., for a function `calculate_sum` in the `MathUtils` class located in `src/helpers/math_helpers.py`, represent it as: 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'.\n\n## Step 3: Analyze the feature\n- Understand the Purpose of the Feature in the Context of the Codebase\n    - Understand what the feature is supposed to do.\n    - Understand why it is needed.\n    - Understand how it fits into the overall functionality of the codebase.\n- Analyze the Feature's Relationship with the Codebase\n    - Identify the key affected components of the feature.\n    - Understand how these components interact with each other and with existing code.\n    - Consider the inputs, outputs, and side effects of the feature.\n\n## Step 4: Locate Areas for Feature Implementation\n- Locate specific files, functions, or lines of code implementing the key part of the requested feature or containing critical information for resolving the issue.\n\n- Think Thoroughly: List multiple potential solutions and consider edge cases that could impact the resolution.\n\n## Output Format for Final Results:\nYour final output should list the locations requiring modification, wrapped with triple backticks ```\nEach location should include the file path, class name (if applicable), function name, or line numbers, ordered by importance.\nYour answer would better include about 5 files.\n\nIMPORTANT FORMAT RULES:\n- Output ONLY the location block, followed by `<finish></finish>`.\n- Do NOT include explanations, bullets, headings, numbering, summaries, or notes before or after the location block.\n- Each file path MUST appear on its own line and end with `.py`.\n- Do NOT write `file.py:QualifiedName` on a single line in the final answer.\n- After a file path line, use separate `line:`, `class:`, and `function:` lines as needed.\n\n### Examples:\n```\nfull_path1/file1.py\nline: 10\nclass: MyClass1\nfunction: my_function1\n\nfull_path2/file2.py\nline: 76\nfunction: MyClass2.my_function2\n\nfull_path3/file3.py\nline: 24\nline: 156\nfunction: my_function3\n```\n\nReturn just the location(s)\n\nNote: Your thinking should be thorough and so it's fine if it's very long.\n\n--- BEGIN PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\nTitle: Validate MD5 checksum of downloaded ARFF in fetch_openml\n\n`fetch_openml` downloads the `data_set_description` from openml.org which includes an `md5_checksum` field. This should correspond to the MD5 checksum of the downloaded ARFF data. We should (optionally?) use `hashlib` to verify that the MD5 matches.\n--- END PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\n\nIMPORTANT: You should ONLY interact with the environment provided to you AND NEVER ASK FOR HUMAN HELP.\nDon't include any lambda functions!\nYou should NOT modify any files!\n"}, {"content": "Let's begin by categorizing and extracting key problem information from the issue description.\n\nRequested feature description:\n- Validate MD5 checksum of downloaded ARFF in `fetch_openml`.\n\nBehavior without this feature:\n- The `fetch_openml` function does not validate the MD5 checksum of the downloaded ARFF data against the `md5_checksum` field in `data_set_description`.\n\nExpected behavior with the feature:\n- Optionally use `hashlib` to verify that the MD5 checksum of the downloaded ARFF data matches the `md5_checksum` field.\n\nAdditional context:\n- `fetch_openml` downloads `data_set_description` which includes `md5_checksum`.\n- Validation should be optional.\n\nNext step is to locate the referenced modules in the scikit-learn package.\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>\n[\"fetch_openml\", \"data_set_description\", \"md5_checksum\"]\n</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_get_data_description_by_id\nsklearn/utils/validation.py:_check_fit_params\nsklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py:fetch_rcv1\nsklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py:fetch_rcv1\nsklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py:fetch_rcv1\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py:fetch_openml\nsklearn/datasets/_samples_generator.py:make_classification\nsklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py:_find_permutation\nsklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py:_inverse_permutation\nsklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py:fetch_rcv1\nsklearn/utils/validation.py:_check_fit_params\nsklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py:fetch_rcv1\nsklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py:fetch_rcv1\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py:fetch_openml\nsklearn/datasets/_samples_generator.py:make_classification\nsklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py:fetch_rcv1\nsklearn/datasets/_samples_generator.py:make_classification\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_get_data_description_by_id\nsklearn/utils/validation.py:_check_fit_params\nsklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py:fetch_rcv1\nsklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py:fetch_rcv1\nsklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py:fetch_rcv1\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py:fetch_openml\nsklearn/datasets/_samples_generator.py:make_classification\nsklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py:_find_permutation\nsklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py:_inverse_permutation\nsklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py:fetch_rcv1\n##Searching for term \"fetch_openml\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:fetch_openml`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `fetch_openml`.\n```\n664 | def fetch_openml(\n665 |     name: Optional[str] = None,\n666 |     *,\n667 |     version: Union[str, int] = 'active',\n668 |     data_id: Optional[int] = None,\n669 |     data_home: Optional[str] = None,\n670 |     target_column: Optional[Union[str, List]] = 'default-target',\n671 |     cache: bool = True,\n672 |     return_X_y: bool = False,\n673 |     as_frame: bool = False\n674 | ):\n675 |     \"\"\"Fetch dataset from openml by name or dataset id.\n676 | \n677 |     Datasets are uniquely identified by either an integer ID or by a\n678 |     combination of name and version (i.e. there might be multiple\n679 |     versions of the 'iris' dataset). Please give either name or data_id\n680 |     (not both). In case a name is given, a version can also be\n681 |     provided.\n682 | \n683 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <openml>`.\n684 | \n685 |     .. versionadded:: 0.20\n686 | \n687 |     .. note:: EXPERIMENTAL\n688 | \n689 |         The API is experimental (particularly the return value structure),\n690 |         and might have small backward-incompatible changes in future releases.\n691 | \n692 |     Parameters\n693 |     ----------\n694 |     name : str, default=None\n695 |         String identifier of the dataset. Note that OpenML can have multiple\n696 |         datasets with the same name.\n697 | \n698 |     version : int or 'active', default='active'\n699 |         Version of the dataset. Can only be provided if also ``name`` is given.\n700 |         If 'active' the oldest version that's still active is used. Since\n701 |         there may be more than one active version of a dataset, and those\n702 |         versions may fundamentally be different from one another, setting an\n703 |         exact version is highly recommended.\n704 | \n705 |     data_id : int, default=None\n706 |         OpenML ID of the dataset. The most specific way of retrieving a\n707 |         dataset. If data_id is not given, name (and potential version) are\n708 |         used to obtain a dataset.\n709 | \n710 |     data_home : str, default=None\n711 |         Specify another download and cache folder for the data sets. By default\n712 |         all scikit-learn data is stored in '~/scikit_learn_data' subfolders.\n713 | \n714 |     target_column : str, list or None, default='default-target'\n715 |         Specify the column name in the data to use as target. If\n716 |         'default-target', the standard target column a stored on the server\n717 |         is used. If ``None``, all columns are returned as data and the\n718 |         target is ``None``. If list (of strings), all columns with these names\n719 |         are returned as multi-target (Note: not all scikit-learn classifiers\n720 |         can handle all types of multi-output combinations)\n721 | \n722 |     cache : bool, default=True\n723 |         Whether to cache downloaded datasets using joblib.\n724 | \n725 |     return_X_y : bool, default=False\n726 |         If True, returns ``(data, target)`` instead of a Bunch object. See\n727 |         below for more information about the `data` and `target` objects.\n728 | \n729 |     as_frame : bool or 'auto', default=False\n730 |         If True, the data is a pandas DataFrame including columns with\n731 |         appropriate dtypes (numeric, string or categorical). The target is\n732 |         a pandas DataFrame or Series depending on the number of target_columns.\n733 |         The Bunch will contain a ``frame`` attribute with the target and the\n734 |         data. If ``return_X_y`` is True, then ``(data, target)`` will be pandas\n735 |         DataFrames or Series as describe above.\n736 |         If as_frame is 'auto', the data and target will be converted to\n737 |         DataFrame or Series as if as_frame is set to True, unless the dataset\n738 |         is stored in sparse format.\n739 | \n740 |     Returns\n741 |     -------\n742 | \n743 |     data : :class:`~sklearn.utils.Bunch`\n744 |         Dictionary-like object, with the following attributes.\n745 | \n746 |         data : np.array, scipy.sparse.csr_matrix of floats, or pandas DataFrame\n747 |             The feature matrix. Categorical features are encoded as ordinals.\n748 |         target : np.array, pandas Series or DataFrame\n749 |             The regression target or classification labels, if applicable.\n750 |             Dtype is float if numeric, and object if categorical. If\n751 |             ``as_frame`` is True, ``target`` is a pandas object.\n752 |         DESCR : str\n753 |             The full description of the dataset\n754 |         feature_names : list\n755 |             The names of the dataset columns\n756 |         target_names: list\n757 |             The names of the target columns\n758 | \n759 |         .. versionadded:: 0.22\n760 | \n761 |         categories : dict or None\n762 |             Maps each categorical feature name to a list of values, such\n763 |             that the value encoded as i is ith in the list. If ``as_frame``\n764 |             is True, this is None.\n765 |         details : dict\n766 |             More metadata from OpenML\n767 |         frame : pandas DataFrame\n768 |             Only present when `as_frame=True`. DataFrame with ``data`` and\n769 |             ``target``.\n770 | \n771 |     (data, target) : tuple if ``return_X_y`` is True\n772 | \n773 |         .. note:: EXPERIMENTAL\n774 | \n775 |             This interface is **experimental** and subsequent releases may\n776 |             change attributes without notice (although there should only be\n777 |             minor changes to ``data`` and ``target``).\n778 | \n779 |         Missing values in the 'data' are represented as NaN's. Missing values\n780 |         in 'target' are represented as NaN's (numerical target) or None\n781 |         (categorical target)\n782 |     \"\"\"\n783 |     data_home = get_data_home(data_home=data_home)\n784 |     data_home = join(data_home, 'openml')\n785 |     if cache is False:\n786 |         # no caching will be applied\n787 |         data_home = None\n788 | \n789 |     # check valid function arguments. data_id XOR (name, version) should be\n790 |     # provided\n791 |     if name is not None:\n792 |         # OpenML is case-insensitive, but the caching mechanism is not\n793 |         # convert all data names (str) to lower case\n794 |         name = name.lower()\n795 |         if data_id is not None:\n796 |             raise ValueError(\n797 |                 \"Dataset data_id={} and name={} passed, but you can only \"\n798 |                 \"specify a numeric data_id or a name, not \"\n799 |                 \"both.\".format(data_id, name))\n800 |         data_info = _get_data_info_by_name(name, version, data_home)\n801 |         data_id = data_info['did']\n802 |     elif data_id is not None:\n803 |         # from the previous if statement, it is given that name is None\n804 |         if version != \"active\":\n805 |             raise ValueError(\n806 |                 \"Dataset data_id={} and version={} passed, but you can only \"\n807 |                 \"specify a numeric data_id or a version, not \"\n808 |                 \"both.\".format(data_id, name))\n809 |     else:\n810 |         raise ValueError(\n811 |             \"Neither name nor data_id are provided. Please provide name or \"\n812 |             \"data_id.\")\n813 | \n814 |     data_description = _get_data_description_by_id(data_id, data_home)\n815 |     if data_description['status'] != \"active\":\n816 |         warn(\"Version {} of dataset {} is inactive, meaning that issues have \"\n817 |              \"been found in the dataset. Try using a newer version from \"\n818 |              \"this URL: {}\".format(\n819 |                 data_description['version'],\n820 |                 data_description['name'],\n821 |                 data_description['url']))\n822 |     if 'error' in data_description:\n823 |         warn(\"OpenML registered a problem with the dataset. It might be \"\n824 |              \"unusable. Error: {}\".format(data_description['error']))\n825 |     if 'warning' in data_description:\n826 |         warn(\"OpenML raised a warning on the dataset. It might be \"\n827 |              \"unusable. Warning: {}\".format(data_description['warning']))\n828 | \n829 |     return_sparse = False\n830 |     if data_description['format'].lower() == 'sparse_arff':\n831 |         return_sparse = True\n832 | \n833 |     if as_frame == 'auto':\n834 |         as_frame = not return_sparse\n835 | \n836 |     if as_frame and return_sparse:\n837 |         raise ValueError('Cannot return dataframe with sparse data')\n838 | \n839 |     # download data features, meta-info about column types\n840 |     features_list = _get_data_features(data_id, data_home)\n841 | \n842 |     if not as_frame:\n843 |         for feature in features_list:\n844 |             if 'true' in (feature['is_ignore'], feature['is_row_identifier']):\n845 |                 continue\n846 |             if feature['data_type'] == 'string':\n847 |                 raise ValueError('STRING attributes are not supported for '\n848 |                                  'array representation. Try as_frame=True')\n849 | \n850 |     if target_column == \"default-target\":\n851 |         # determines the default target based on the data feature results\n852 |         # (which is currently more reliable than the data description;\n853 |         # see issue: https://github.com/openml/OpenML/issues/768)\n854 |         target_columns = [feature['name'] for feature in features_list\n855 |                           if feature['is_target'] == 'true']\n856 |     elif isinstance(target_column, str):\n857 |         # for code-simplicity, make target_column by default a list\n858 |         target_columns = [target_column]\n859 |     elif target_column is None:\n860 |         target_columns = []\n861 |     elif isinstance(target_column, list):\n862 |         target_columns = target_column\n863 |     else:\n864 |         raise TypeError(\"Did not recognize type of target_column\"\n865 |                         \"Should be str, list or None. Got: \"\n866 |                         \"{}\".format(type(target_column)))\n867 |     data_columns = _valid_data_column_names(features_list,\n868 |    