\n683 |     easier to test multiple estimators.\n684 | \n685 |     Checks are categorised into the following groups:\n686 | \n687 |     - API checks: a set of checks to ensure API compatibility with scikit-learn.\n688 |       Refer to https://scikit-learn.org/dev/developers/develop.html a requirement of\n689 |       scikit-learn estimators.\n690 |     - legacy: a set of checks which gradually will be grouped into other categories.\n691 | \n692 |     Parameters\n693 |     ----------\n694 |     estimator : estimator object\n695 |         Estimator instance to check.\n696 | \n697 |     generate_only : bool, default=False\n698 |         When `False`, checks are evaluated when `check_estimator` is called.\n699 |         When `True`, `check_estimator` returns a generator that yields\n700 |         (estimator, check) tuples. The check is run by calling\n701 |         `check(estimator)`.\n702 | \n703 |         .. versionadded:: 0.22\n704 | \n705 |         .. deprecated:: 1.6\n706 |             `generate_only` will be removed in 1.8. Use\n707 |             :func:`~sklearn.utils.estimator_checks.estimator_checks_generator` instead.\n708 | \n709 |     legacy : bool, default=True\n710 |         Whether to include legacy checks. Over time we remove checks from this category\n711 |         and move them into their specific category.\n712 | \n713 |         .. versionadded:: 1.6\n714 | \n715 |     expected_failed_checks : dict, default=None\n716 |         A dictionary of the form::\n717 | \n718 |             {\n719 |                 \"check_name\": \"this check is expected to fail because ...\",\n720 |             }\n721 | \n722 |         Where `\"check_name\"` is the name of the check, and `\"my reason\"` is why\n723 |         the check fails.\n724 | \n725 |         .. versionadded:: 1.6\n726 | \n727 |     on_skip : \"warn\", None, default=\"warn\"\n728 |         This parameter controls what happens when a check is skipped.\n729 | \n730 |         - \"warn\": A :class:`~sklearn.exceptions.SkipTestWarning` is logged\n731 |           and running tests continue.\n732 |         - None: No warning is logged and running tests continue.\n733 | \n734 |         .. versionadded:: 1.6\n735 | \n736 |     on_fail : {\"raise\", \"warn\"}, None, default=\"raise\"\n737 |         This parameter controls what happens when a check fails.\n738 | \n739 |         - \"raise\": The exception raised by the first failing check is raised and\n740 |           running tests are aborted. This does not included tests that are expected\n741 |           to fail.\n742 |         - \"warn\": A :class:`~sklearn.exceptions.EstimatorCheckFailedWarning` is logged\n743 |           and running tests continue.\n744 |         - None: No exception is raised and no warning is logged.\n745 | \n746 |         Note that if ``on_fail != \"raise\"``, no exception is raised, even if the checks\n747 |         fail. You'd need to inspect the return result of ``check_estimator`` to check\n748 |         if any checks failed.\n749 | \n750 |         .. versionadded:: 1.6\n751 | \n752 |     callback : callable, or None, default=None\n753 |         This callback will be called with the estimator and the check name,\n754 |         the exception (if any), the status of the check (xfail, failed, skipped,\n755 |         passed), and the reason for the expected failure if the check is\n756 |         expected to fail. The callable's signature needs to be::\n757 | \n758 |             def callback(\n759 |                 estimator,\n760 |                 check_name: str,\n761 |                 exception: Exception,\n762 |                 status: Literal[\"xfail\", \"failed\", \"skipped\", \"passed\"],\n763 |                 expected_to_fail: bool,\n764 |                 expected_to_fail_reason: str,\n765 |             )\n766 | \n767 |         ``callback`` cannot be provided together with ``on_fail=\"raise\"``.\n768 | \n769 |         .. versionadded:: 1.6\n770 | \n771 |     Returns\n772 |     -------\n773 |     test_results : list\n774 |         List of dictionaries with the results of the failing tests, of the form::\n775 | \n776 |             {\n777 |                 \"estimator\": estimator,\n778 |                 \"check_name\": check_name,\n779 |                 \"exception\": exception,\n780 |                 \"status\": status (one of \"xfail\", \"failed\", \"skipped\", \"passed\"),\n781 |                 \"expected_to_fail\": expected_to_fail,\n782 |                 \"expected_to_fail_reason\": expected_to_fail_reason,\n783 |             }\n784 | \n785 |     estimator_checks_generator : generator\n786 |         Generator that yields (estimator, check) tuples. Returned when\n787 |         `generate_only=True`.\n788 | \n789 |         ..\n790 |             TODO(1.8): remove return value\n791 | \n792 |         .. deprecated:: 1.6\n793 |             ``generate_only`` will be removed in 1.8. Use\n794 |             :func:`~sklearn.utils.estimator_checks.estimator_checks_generator` instead.\n795 | \n796 |     Raises\n797 |     ------\n798 |     Exception\n799 |         If ``on_fail=\"raise\"``, the exception raised by the first failing check is\n800 |         raised and running tests are aborted.\n801 | \n802 |         Note that if ``on_fail != \"raise\"``, no exception is raised, even if the checks\n803 |         fail. You'd need to inspect the return result of ``check_estimator`` to check\n804 |         if any checks failed.\n805 | \n806 |     See Also\n807 |     --------\n808 |     parametrize_with_checks : Pytest specific decorator for parametrizing estimator\n809 |         checks.\n810 |     estimator_checks_generator : Generator that yields (estimator, check) tuples.\n811 | \n812 |     Examples\n813 |     --------\n814 |     >>> from sklearn.utils.estimator_checks import check_estimator\n815 |     >>> from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\n816 |     >>> check_estimator(LogisticRegression())\n817 |     [...]\n818 |     \"\"\"\n819 |     if isinstance(es