h the following attributes.\n822 | \n823 |         data : {ndarray, dataframe} of shape (1797, 64)\n824 |             The flattened data matrix. If `as_frame=True`, `data` will be\n825 |             a pandas DataFrame.\n826 |         target: {ndarray, Series} of shape (1797,)\n827 |             The classification target. If `as_frame=True`, `target` will be\n828 |             a pandas Series.\n829 |         feature_names: list\n830 |             The names of the dataset columns.\n831 |         target_names: list\n832 |             The names of target classes.\n833 | \n834 |             .. versionadded:: 0.20\n835 | \n836 |         frame: DataFrame of shape (1797, 65)\n837 |             Only present when `as_frame=True`. DataFrame with `data` and\n838 |             `target`.\n839 | \n840 |             .. versionadded:: 0.23\n841 |         images: {ndarray} of shape (1797, 8, 8)\n842 |             The raw image data.\n843 |         DESCR: str\n844 |             The full description of the dataset.\n845 | \n846 |     (data, target) : tuple if ``return_X_y`` is True\n847 | \n848 |         .. versionadded:: 0.18\n849 | \n850 |     This is a copy of the test set of the UCI ML hand-written digits datasets\n851 |     https://archive.ics.uci.edu/ml/datasets/Optical+Recognition+of+Handwritten+Digits\n852 | \n853 |     Examples\n854 |     --------\n855 |     To load the data and visualize the images::\n856 | \n857 |         >>> from sklearn.datasets import load_digits\n858 |         >>> digits = load_digits()\n859 |         >>> print(digits.data.shape)\n860 |         (1797, 64)\n861 |         >>> import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n862 |         >>> plt.gray()\n863 |         >>> plt.matshow(digits.images[0])\n864 |         <...>\n865 |         >>> plt.show()\n866 |     \"\"\"\n867 | \n868 |     data, fdescr = load_gzip_compressed_csv_data(\n869 |         data_file_name=\"digits.csv.gz\", descr_file_name=\"digits.rst\", delimiter=\",\"\n870 |     )\n871 | \n872 |     target = data[:, -1].astype(int, copy=False)\n873 |     flat_data = data[:, :-1]\n874 |     images = flat_data.view()\n875 |     images.shape = (-1, 8, 8)\n876 | \n877 |     if n_class < 10:\n878 |         idx = target < n_class\n879 |         flat_data, target = flat_data[idx], target[idx]\n880 |         images = images[idx]\n881 | \n882 |     feature_names = [\n883 |         \"pixel_{}_{}\".format(row_idx, col_idx)\n884 |         for row_idx in range(8)\n885 |         for col_idx in range(8)\n886 |     ]\n887 | \n888 |     frame = None\n889 |     target_columns = [\n890 |         \"target\",\n891 |     ]\n892 |     if as_frame:\n893 |         frame, flat_data, target = _convert_data_dataframe(\n894 |             \"load_digits\", flat_data, target, feature_names, target_columns\n895 |         )\n896 | \n897 |     if return_X_y:\n898 |         return flat_data, target\n899 | \n900 |     return Bunch(\n901 |         data=flat_data,\n902 |         target=target,\n903 |         frame=frame,\n904 |         feature_names=feature_names,\n905 |         target_names=np.arange(10),\n906 |         images=images,\n907 |         DESCR=fdescr,\n908 |     )\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_base.py:load_linnerud`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1008 | def load_linnerud(*, return_X_y=False, as_frame=False):\n1009 |     \"\"\"Load and return the physical excercise linnerud dataset.\n1010 | \n1011 |     This dataset is suitable for multi-ouput regression tasks.\n1012 | \n1013 |     ==============   ============================\n1014 |     Samples total    20\n1015 |     Dimensionality   3 (for both data and target)\n1016 |     Features         integer\n1017 |     Targets          integer\n1018 |     ==============   ============================\n1019 | \n1020 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <linnerrud_dataset>`.\n1021 | \n1022 |     Parameters\n1023 |     ----------\n1024 |     return_X_y : bool, default=False\n1025 |         If True, returns `