uments will be passed to matplotlib's `plot`.\n150 | \n151 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <visualizations>`.\n152 | \n153 |     Parameters\n154 |     ----------\n155 |     estimator : estimator instance\n156 |         Fitted classifier or a fitted :class:`~sklearn.pipeline.Pipeline`\n157 |         in which the last estimator is a classifier.\n158 | \n159 |     X : {array-like, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n160 |         Input values.\n161 | \n162 |     y : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n163 |         Target values.\n164 | \n165 |     sample_weight : array-like of shape (n_samples,), default=None\n166 |         Sample weights.\n167 | \n168 |     drop_intermediate : boolean, default=True\n169 |         Whether to drop some suboptimal thresholds which would not appear\n170 |         on a plotted ROC curve. This is useful in order to create lighter\n171 |         ROC curves.\n172 | \n173 |     response_method : {'predict_proba', 'decision_function', 'auto'} \\\n174 |     default='auto'\n175 |         Specifies whether to use :term:`predict_proba` or\n176 |         :term:`decision_function` as the target response. If set to 'auto',\n177 |         :term:`predict_proba` is tried first and if it does not exist\n178 |         :term:`decision_function` is tried next.\n179 | \n180 |     name : str, default=None\n181 |         Name of ROC Curve for labeling. If `None`, use the name of the\n182 |         estimator.\n183 | \n184 |     ax : matplotlib axes, default=None\n185 |         Axes object to plot on. If `None`, a new figure and axes is created.\n186 | \n187 |     pos_label : str or int, default=None\n188 |         The class considered as the positive class when computing the roc auc\n189 |         metrics. By default, `estimators.classes_[1]` is considered\n190 |         as the positive class.\n191 | \n192 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n193 | \n194 |     Returns\n195 |     -------\n196 |     display : :class:`~sklearn.metrics.RocCurveDisplay`\n197 |         Object that stores computed values.\n198 | \n199 |     See Also\n200 |     --------\n201 |     roc_curve : Compute Receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve.\n202 |     RocCurveDisplay : ROC Curve visualization.\n203 |     roc_auc_score : Compute the area under the ROC curve.\n204 | \n205 |     Examples\n206 |     --------\n207 |     >>> import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n208 |     >>> from sklearn import datasets, metrics, model_selection, svm\n209 |     >>> X, y = datasets.make_classification(random_state=0)\n210 |     >>> X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = model_selection.train_test_split(\n211 |     ...     X, y, random_state=0)\n212 |     >>> clf = svm.SVC(random_state=0)\n213 |     >>> clf.fit(X_train, y_train)\n214 |     SVC(random_state=0)\n215 |     >>> metrics.plot_roc_curve(clf, X_test, y_test)\n216 |     <...>\n217 |     >>> plt.show()\n218 |     \"\"\"\n219 |     check_matplotlib_support(\"plot_roc_curve\")\n220 | \n221 |     y_pred, pos_label = _get_response(\n222 |         X, estimator, response_method, pos_label=pos_label\n223 |     )\n224 | \n225 |     fpr, tpr, _ = roc_curve(\n226 |         y,\n227 |         y_pred,\n228 |         pos_label=pos_label,\n229 |         sample_weight=sample_weight,\n230 |         drop_intermediate=drop_intermediate,\n231 |     )\n232 |     roc_auc = auc(fpr, tpr)\n233 | \n234 |     name = estimator.__class__.__name__ if name is None else name\n235 | \n236 |     viz = RocCurveDisplay(\n237 |         fpr=fpr, tpr=tpr, roc_auc=roc_auc, estimator_name=name, pos_label=pos_label\n238 |     )\n239 | \n240 |     return viz.plot(ax=ax, name=name, **kwargs)\n```\n\nFound file `sklearn/metrics/_plot/roc_curve.py`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nHint: Search `sklearn/metrics/_plot/roc_curve.py` for the full content if needed.\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"class .*CurveDisplay(\", \"class .*Display( class .*CurveDisplay(\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `sklearn/metrics/_plot/det_c